// library domain -- the AI-facing artifact-label tool surface.
//
// artifactAddLabel / artifactRemoveLabel let an agent put a label on (or
// take one off) a Library artifact the user already has -- the assistant
// counterpart to the portal's own label editor. Both ride the SAME builtin
// (dsl/library/builtins.memql: libraryAddArtifactLabel /
// libraryRemoveArtifactLabel), which is also @sdk-exposed so the portal
// calls it directly through the generated client SDK. One implementation
// serves both callers because, unlike editDocument's user/assistant split,
// there is no attribution difference between an agent adding a label and a
// person adding one -- a label carries no authorKind. See the
// artifacts-labels design doc, D2.
//
// The handler reaches the builtin through the `function` type -- there is
// no "builtin" handler type. The target is resolved at load, so a name
// typo here refuses boot rather than failing the first time an agent calls
// the tool.
//
// Ownership needs no new mechanism: the builtin loads the artifact row
// under a system actor, then acts under a synthetic actor derived from the
// ROW's own ownerUserId (mirrors handleEditDocument's shape exactly), so
// an agent acting in a user's session can only ever label an artifact that
// user can already read.

/// Put a label on an artifact so the person can tell later what it was for. Idempotent -- adding a
/// label that is already there succeeds and changes nothing. Use this when the user asks to
/// label / tag / categorize an artifact (a document, a generated deliverable) they already have.
@handler(type="function", name="libraryAddArtifactLabel")
@allowedRoles("assistant", "specialist")
@executionTime("fast")
tool artifactAddLabel {
  artifactId  string!  @description("Id of the artifact to label -- the v1:library:artifact row id.")
  label       string!  @description("The label to add. Short and human-readable -- a project or topic name, not a sentence.")
}

/// Remove a label from an artifact. Idempotent -- removing a label that is not there succeeds and
/// changes nothing. Use this when the user asks to un-label / remove a tag from an artifact they
/// already have.
@handler(type="function", name="libraryRemoveArtifactLabel")
@allowedRoles("assistant", "specialist")
@executionTime("fast")
tool artifactRemoveLabel {
  artifactId  string!  @description("Id of the artifact to remove the label from -- the v1:library:artifact row id.")
  label       string!  @description("The label to remove. Must match an existing label exactly.")
}

// ---- the search + train tools (memql#4342) ----
//
// artifactSearch lets an agent look through the person's own Library by
// meaning; artifactTrain lets it put a file into a knowledge domain when
// the person asks. Both ride the SAME builtins the portal calls through
// the generated SDK (dsl/library/builtins.memql:
// librarySimilarArtifacts / libraryTrainFile), for the label pair's
// reason: there is no attribution difference between an agent searching
// and a person searching.
//
// Reached through the `function` handler type -- there is no "builtin"
// handler type -- and resolved at load, so a name typo here refuses boot
// rather than failing the first time an agent calls the tool.
//
// AUTHORIZATION NEEDS NO NEW MECHANISM, AND THE TWO TOOLS GET IT FROM
// DIFFERENT PLACES. artifactSearch reads under the AGENT'S SESSION USER:
// the capability filters chunks on the acting user's id and re-reads each
// artifact through an owner-gated query, so an agent can only ever surface
// files the person it is acting for can already open. artifactTrain adds
// the domain half on top: writing is refused unless the cluster's
// knowledge-domain authorizer confirms the user may write there (a
// cluster owner always may). An agent therefore cannot train a user's
// file into a domain that user could not train it into themselves.

/// Search the person's Library by meaning and get back whole artifacts, best match first. Use this
/// when they refer to something they have -- "the pricing deck", "that PDF about onboarding", "what
/// did my notes say about X" -- instead of asking them to find it. Returns each artifact's id,
/// title, kind, summary, labels and the matching snippet, so you can cite what you found. Search by
/// a phrase with `text`, or pass `artifactId` to find things like one you already have.
@handler(type="function", name="librarySimilarArtifacts")
@allowedRoles("assistant", "specialist")
@executionTime("fast")
tool artifactSearch {
  text        string  @description("What to look for, in the person's own words. A phrase or a sentence works better than a keyword. Set this OR artifactId.")
  artifactId  string  @description("Find artifacts similar to this one -- the v1:library:artifact row id. Set this OR text.")
  limit       int     @description("How many artifacts to return. Defaults to 5; ask for more only when the person wants a survey rather than an answer.")
}

/// Train one of the person's Library files into a knowledge domain, so agents can draw on it later.
/// Use this ONLY when they ask for it -- uploading a file does not train it, and training is a
/// decision they make about their own material. Say which file and which domain you are about to
/// use before you call it. Refused when the file is not theirs, when the file has no readable text,
/// or when they may not write to that domain.
@handler(type="function", name="libraryTrainFile")
@allowedRoles("assistant", "specialist")
@executionTime("slow")
tool artifactTrain {
  fileId    string!  @description("The v1:library:file id of the file to train -- the fileId on the artifact, not the artifact id.")
  domainId  string!  @description("Id of the knowledge domain to train it into. Use one the person named or picked; do not guess a domain.")
}
